https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5wSztvWhx14

section 1: setup
step 1: initiate npm
create a folder locally and open it in VS code
open terminal in VS code and run $ npm init -y, it will create package.json file (-y: select yes on all the upcoming questions)

step 2: install Playwright test
$ npm init playwright@latest

step 3: create tests file
create a file under 'tests' folder and name it in this format 'blabla.spec.js' ('blabla.spec.ts' if use typescript), that's where we add tests in


section 2: create tests
run in VS code $ npx playwright codegen <url that you are testing>
it would pop up a playwright inspector window, as we click on the webpage, it would record all our behaviours and generate test scripts in it;
it would pop up a browser window as well which is the application page we are testing, click 'assert visibility' or 'assert texts' or 'assert values' icons 
to add assertions into the scripts, click stop recording icon once finish;
copy the scripts from playwright inspector into the test file 'blabla.spec.js', save it, then ctrl+C in terminal.

in the scripts we can also manually add scripts as below:
const newTodo = page.locator('new-todo');
await newTodo.fill('task1');
await newTodo.press('Enter');

const items = page.locator('some texts');
await expect(items).toHaveCount(3);

//mark second item complete
await items.nth(1).locator('toggle').check();
await expect(page.locator('walawala')).toHaveCount(1);

//delete third item (hover + click destroy)
await.items.nth(2).hover();
await items.nth(2).locator('.destroy').click({ force: true });
await.expect(items).toHaveCount(2);

await.page.screenshot({ path: 'sth.png' });


section 3: run tests
$ npx playwright test
it would generate a test report in http://localhost:9323
in case the report doesnt pop up, add --reporter=html to generate it, eg. $ npx playwright test --headed --reporter=html
then show it: $ npx playwright show-report

best practice:
add tag in this line of the test file: test('test', async ({ page }) => {
test('test some app @tagishere', async ({ page }) => {
then run $ npx playwright test --headed --grep @tagishere
it would only run the tests we specified, excluding the example.spec.js file that auto-generated while installing playwright
run $ npx playwright show-report if it doesnt pop up

note: run in headed mode means displaying the browser window while running tests instead of hiding it

if we want to specify a browser (say, Chrome) to run the tests: add --project=chromium
eg. $ npx playwright test --headed --grep @tagishere --project=chromium
this name has to be the same as 'projects' part in playwright.config.js file

if we want to slow down the running process to see it more clear, just add these scripts in 'use' part under 'trace' in playwright.config.js file
launchOptions: {
    slowMo: 300,
}

also running tests in playwright ui mode is useful for debugging, say, $ npx playwright test --ui


section 4: take screenshot on failure
example of 'use' part in playwright.config.js file:
use: {
    browserName: 'chromium',
    headless: false;
    screenshot: 'only-on-failure';
    trace: 'retain-on-failure',        //keeps trace for debugging
    video: 'retain-on-failure'         //record video if test fails
},


